        "prompt": "Explain why she is incorrect.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "The gradient is $1$, so the equation is $y=x$.",
          "worked": "Using $(0,0)$ and $(4,4)$:\n$$\\begin{aligned}m&=\\frac{\\text{rise}}{\\text{run}}\\\\m&=\\frac{4}{4}\\\\m&=1\\end{aligned}$$\nThe $y$-intercept is $0$, so $y=x$, rather than $y=\\frac12x$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 18
      },
      {
        "id": "p26-q9-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "What makes this question tricky?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "The $y$-axis and $x$-axis scales are different.",
          "worked": "Each tick interval represents $1$ on the $x$-axis and $2$ on the $y$-axis. Use the coordinate values to calculate rise and run.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 14
      }
